Co-built with another engineer.

## The problem

Running creators meant a spreadsheet for the schedule, a separate tool for posting, and
manual copy and paste for anything that went to more than one person. None of those
steps were difficult. There were just a lot of them, and every handoff between tools
was somewhere for work to quietly go missing.

## One data model, two workflows

Scheduling and mass messaging were the things the team did constantly, so the data model
was built around them from the start rather than fitted around them later. A post has an
explicit lifecycle, and the state it is in is a real field rather than something inferred
from whether a timestamp happens to be null. That distinction sounds pedantic right up
until someone asks why a post did not go out and you need to answer precisely.

## Typed end to end

React and TypeScript on the front, FastAPI behind it, with response shapes and component
props kept in agreement. On a small team moving quickly, this is the cheapest way to
discover that a field was renamed. The compiler tells you before a user does.

## Living on other people's APIs

Scheduling a post is only useful if it actually reaches the platform, so the service
talks directly to the social media APIs rather than handing someone a reminder to go and
do it. Each platform has its own auth, its own rate limits and its own opinion about what
a valid post looks like, so the integration layer normalises them behind one internal
interface. When a platform changes its rules, there is one place to fix.

Google APIs carry the other half. Spreadsheets was already where the team kept plans and
reporting, so rather than forcing everyone into a new tool the platform reads and writes
those sheets directly, and Drive holds the assets that go out with a post. Meeting people
in the tools they already trust removed more friction than any feature I could have added
inside the app.

## Mass messaging is a partial failure problem

Sending to many recipients never fully succeeds or fully fails. Some sends go through,
some get rate limited, some bounce, and the person who pressed the button needs to know
which is which. Sends are queued and tracked per recipient, so retrying hits the ones
that did not land instead of duplicating the ones that did. Nobody wants to explain a
double send.

## Stack

React and TypeScript on the front, FastAPI behind it, integrated with the social media
platform APIs and Google APIs (Spreadsheets and Drive), deployed on AWS.
